## MUST A — closed, and the routing union is the right shape

The ladder now asks "is this a playwright run?" at clause 3 and routes a yes
**unconditionally** into the count and matcher clauses, with the token clause
demoted to 7. I re-ran the new kit:

```
   ADV B 3-failed + 'not ok 1 - setup'  refuse  want=refuse  playwright reported 3 failed tests; a behaviour's red is exactly one
   ADV C 3-failed + AssertionError      refuse  want=refuse  playwright reported 3 failed tests; a behaviour's red is exactly one
   NEW F real red 02 + server AssertionError ACCEPT  want=ACCEPT
…
22 transcripts, 0 disagreements
```

My two round-1 acceptances now refuse with the count reason, and the converse
holds: case F takes the **real** body of `studio/ci/reds/W-110/02.log` and
appends a server `AssertionError`, and it is still accepted. That is the
assertion that proves the routing refuses the near miss without breaking the
red it exists to admit, and 4(k) carries it in the same row rather than in a
separate one. Good.

I checked the kit implements the brief's ladder faithfully rather than a
different one that happens to agree: clause order, all four constants and
`[...output.matchAll(PW_SUMMARY)].at(-1)` are identical to `:306` onward.

**The union's two anchors both hold against real reporter output**, measured
by me on the actual red bodies rather than taken from the brief:

```
=== red 02 ===        === red 03 ===
3:Running 1 test using 1 worker          3:Running 1 test using 1 worker
10:    expect(locator).toHaveCount(expected) failed   12:    expect(received).toBeGreaterThan(expected)
36:  1 failed                            32:  1 failed
```

So W-110's two blocked reds carry **both** anchors and a matching banner, and
they reach acceptance through the playwright branch — not through the token
clause, which is the unsafe direction the brief names. Note the real text is
singular on both counts (`1 test`, `1 worker`): `tests?`/`workers?` handles it,
and had the regex been written plural-only the routing would have fallen
through to clause 7 on exactly the transcripts this opus exists for. The
`launch` 4-MiB-per-stream justification for the union is sound and the brief
states which direction each anchor covers.

**The declared cost checks out independently.** Case J — a TAP transcript
printing `1 failed` at line start — is routed to the playwright branch and
refused. I verified the premise myself rather than accepting "measured,
nothing in the repo does it": a real failing `node --test-reporter=tap` run
produces no line-start `N failed` (its summary is `# fail 1`), and none of
W-110's six TAP reds matches the anchor. Fail-closed, recoverable, pinned in
both directions by 4(m), and *Out of scope* forbids narrowing the anchor to
get it back. The right way to accept a cost.

## MUST B — closed, and the fix surfaced a real seam

The sentinel-everywhere option is taken, and the brief found the thing my
finding implied but did not name: `builder-run.ts:229` hands the runner a
**closed** environment, so a sentinel in the test file alone could not reach
`browserCache()` through the `run(...)` path. Verified against the real line:

```
229:      env: { PATH: process.env["PATH"] ?? TOOL_PATH, LANG: "C.UTF-8", LC_ALL: "C.UTF-8", TZ: "UTC", ...(process.env["TMPDIR"] ? { TMPDIR: process.env["TMPDIR"] } : {}) },
```

The proposed addition is the same conditional-spread shape the line already
uses for `TMPDIR`, `isAbsolute` is already imported (`builder-run.ts:5`), and
the `:227-231` block reference is accurate. `withTmp` (`:258-267`) is indeed
the file's established save-and-restore pattern, so pinning the sentinel there
and in `RUNNER_ENV` covers both dispatch paths. Behaviour 2(f) is the row that
makes this worth having: it asserts the cell's `/browsers` holds the
**sentinel's** file and nothing from the developer's real cache, so the test
cannot silently pass by reading `~/.cache/ms-playwright`. The Acceptance
addition — "green here and red on CI is not acceptance" — is the right standing
rule to have written down.

The brief also corrected me: I named the `npm test` job `officina`; it is
`gates` (`ci.yml:15`, `npm test` at `:29`), with `npx playwright install` only
in `web-e2e` at `:69`. Verified — the brief is right and I was wrong.

